      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find out how much weight Terrell can lift with two 15-pound weights.\nFirst, we need to calculate the total weight Terrell can lift with two 20-pound weights.\nSince he lifts two weights, the total weight he can lift with two 20-pound weights is:\n2 * 20 pounds = 40 pounds per set\nSince he lifts 12 times, the total weight he can lift with two 20-pound weights is:\n40 pounds per set * 12 sets = 480 pounds\nNow, we need to find out how many times Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights in order to lift the same total weight.\nSince he lifts two weights, the total weight he can lift with two 15-pound weights is:\n2 * 15 pounds = 30 pounds per set\nLet x be the number of sets that Terrell needs to lift two 15-pound weights in order to lift 480 pounds.\n30 pounds per set * x sets = 480 pounds\n30x = 480\nTo solve for x, we divide both sides of the equation by 30.\nx = 16\nTherefore, Terrell must lift two 15-pound weights 16 times in order to lift the same total weight.\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{16}$.",
