    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights, let's first set up an equation that represents the total weight lifted for each scenario.\n\nFor 20-pound weights:\nLet x be the number of times he lifts these.\n(20 # of lbs * x) = 20x\n\nFor 15-pound weights:\nLet y be the number of times he lifts these.\n(15 # of lbs * y)\n\nWe want the total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights to be equal to the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights:\n20x = 15y\n\nNow we must solve for y (how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equation:\n20x = 15y\n\nNext, we want to isolate y on one side of the equation:\nDivide both sides by 15 to get: \n(y) = (20x) / (15)\n\nNow let's simplify:\ny = (4/3)x\n\nSo, for every 3 lifts of the 20-pound weights, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights 4 times. The general formula for any number of x lifts of the 20-pound weights is 4 times as long. For example, if Terrell lifts 18 lifts of the 20-pound weights, he would need to lift the 15-pound weights 66 times (18 lifts * 4 lifts/18).\n\nFinal Answer:\nTerrell must lift the 15-pound weights \\boxed{6} times to match the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights.",
